      "problem": "If $K=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 16 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]$, then ${K}^{6}=$",
      "worked_solution": "Computing ${K}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{2} & =K\\cdot K \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 16 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-4 & 16 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since ${K}^{2}=O,$ the matrix $K$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$ \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${K}^{6}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{6} & ={K}^{2}\\cdot {K}^{4} \\\\  & =O\\cdot {K}^{4} \\\\  & =O\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{K}^{6}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
