      "knowledge_point": "Finding a Power of an Idempotent Matrix",
      "problem": "If $Z=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}]$, then ${Z}^{5}=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we compute ${Z}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Z}^{2} & =Z\\cdot Z \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${Z}^{2}=Z,$ so $Z$ is an idempotent matrix. \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${Z}^{5}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Z}^{5} & ={Z}^{2}\\cdot {Z}^{2}\\cdot Z \\\\  & =Z\\cdot Z\\cdot Z \\\\  & ={Z}^{2}\\cdot Z \\\\  & =Z\\cdot Z \\\\  & ={Z}^{2} \\\\  & =Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Z}^{5}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
